{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1676 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2e54c8c2506e686414aefbb3d6e31294fd88e0b489a74388a1bc86719825a8a4" :edb/db-before-t 1675 :edb/db-after-t 1676 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534988 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:27:11.129Z" 13194139534988 true] [17592186229115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325191 13194139534988 true] [17592186229116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325202 13194139534988 true] [17592186229117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325188 13194139534988 true] [17592186229117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325195 13194139534988 true] [17592186229117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325198 13194139534988 true] [17592186325188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325189 13194139534988 true] [17592186325188 1089 "q-332938" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534988 true] [17592186325188 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325188 1113 #uuid "0b0f029e-226e-5aa8-b0f8-aab45fe31a03" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325188 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325190 13194139534988 true] [17592186325189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534988 true] [17592186325189 1041 "field-1" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325190 13194139534988 true] [17592186325189 1110 #uuid "f7e92480-dad9-5345-a383-6933341e3425" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325190 1034 "(15x+1)e^{5x}" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534988 true] [17592186325190 1111 #uuid "73223c6b-80f8-5f93-9793-fb86cd7d5397" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325191 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325192 13194139534988 true] [17592186325191 1089 "q-333147" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325191 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534988 true] [17592186325191 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325191 1113 #uuid "6698921c-b2b9-50d3-ac86-7d515f9b0b54" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325191 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =8\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =-5,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =8\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325193 13194139534988 true] [17592186325192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325194 13194139534988 true] [17592186325192 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534988 true] [17592186325192 1041 "field-1" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325193 13194139534988 true] [17592186325192 1110 #uuid "1310a78b-30d2-5e03-be94-9541f6c35e91" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325193 1034 "8xe^{-4x}-e^x" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534988 true] [17592186325193 1111 #uuid "07b4a43e-7b1e-5f42-b952-3fc4f05b2e6e" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325194 1034 "8xe^{-4x}+e^x" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534988 true] [17592186325194 1111 #uuid "5ebd2d9b-c460-5687-b324-dfac8b95a5b4" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325195 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325196 13194139534988 true] [17592186325195 1089 "q-333160" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325195 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534988 true] [17592186325195 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325195 1113 #uuid "002b88e2-78e7-53d4-907a-a035eccfda11" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325195 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}) & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325197 13194139534988 true] [17592186325196 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534988 true] [17592186325196 1041 "field-1" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325196 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325197 13194139534988 true] [17592186325196 1110 #uuid "47fe8f3a-0956-532c-8a2f-0ee306a9271f" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325197 1034 "(12x+1)e^{-3x}" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534988 true] [17592186325197 1111 #uuid "ba81125d-c905-5b57-af0b-715c31a4a497" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325198 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325199 13194139534988 true] [17592186325198 1089 "q-333171" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325198 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534988 true] [17592186325198 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325198 1113 #uuid "60b4feb8-069e-5bff-99c3-ce2e18b5a210" 13194139534988 true] [17592186325198 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x})-3(\\alpha {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-3)\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$"